# Never commit a real person's payload. This repository is public and git
# history is permanent. A QR code is a container for exactly the things people
# do not want published: a Wi-Fi password, a phone number, a home address on a
# contact card, an IBAN, a link to a private document. The inspector in this
# package exists because those things are in there.
#
# There are no binary fixtures at all, which is why there is no rule for them
# here. Every test image is drawn in code by this package's own renderer, every
# payload is synthetic or published in the specification it tests, and a wrong
# expectation shows up in the diff rather than hiding inside a file nobody can
# read.
#
# Photographs of real codes, printed test sheets photographed on a phone, and
# anything generated to check this package against other readers belong in
# scratch/, which is ignored.
scratch/
*.private.*
real-*
